 "drivingQuestion": "Why does the verse say 'whose neck was broken'?",
 "outcome": "not_dialectical",
 "outcomeNote": "A baraita's exposition of an apparently superfluous word",
 "continuesQuestionOf": null,
 "segments": [
  {
   "id": "sotah.46b.8",
   "daf": "46b",
   "sentenceId": 7,
   "he": "זִקְנֵי הָעִיר רוֹחֲצִין יְדֵיהֶן כּוּ׳. תָּנוּ רַבָּנַן: ״וְכׇל זִקְנֵי הָעִיר הָהִיא הַקְּרֹבִים אֶל הֶחָלָל יִרְחֲצוּ אֶת יְדֵיהֶם עַל הָעֶגְלָה הָעֲרוּפָה בַנָּחַל״. שֶׁאֵין תַּלְמוּד לוֹמַר ״הָעֲרוּפָה״, וּמָה תַּלְמוּד לוֹמַר ״הָעֲרוּפָה״ — עַל מְקוֹם עֲרִיפָתָהּ שֶׁל עֶגְלָה.",
   "en": "§ The mishna taught that «the Elders of the city» would then «wash their hands. The Sages taught:» With regard to the verse: «“And all the Elders of that city, who are nearest to the slain man, shall wash their hands over the heifer whose neck was broken in the valley”» (Deuteronomy 21:6), one might have thought «that» there is «no» need for «the verse to state: “Whose neck was broken,”» because there is no heifer mentioned other than the one whose neck was broken. «And what» is the meaning when «the verse states: “Whose neck was broken”?» It serves to teach us that they wash their hands «over the place where the heifer’s neck was broken.»"
  }
 ],
